Title = {Socio-economic embeddedness of importance-performance analysis of
   single-use plastic limitation policies: policy perspectives in plastic
   segregation for the circular economy},
Journal = {WASTE MANAGEMENT BULLETIN},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {4},
Number = {3},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {The escalating issue of single-use plastic waste in rapidly urbanizing
   cities necessitates policy frameworks that integrate behavioral
   participation with transitions toward a circular economy. This study
   investigates public perceptions of policies aimed at limiting single-use
   plastics and the determinants of willingness to engage in plastic
   segregation programs in Jakarta, Indonesia. Specifically, the research
   seeks to identify critical policy gaps and socio-economic factors that
   influence public participation in plastic reduction initiatives. A
   structured survey was administered, which included a 50-respondent
   pre-test followed by a sample of 500 residents in Jakarta, with data
   analyzed using Importance-Performance Analysis (IPA), paired sample
   t-tests, and probit-logit regression models within an adaptive capacity
   framework encompassing dimensions of assets, flexibility, organization,
   learning, and agency. The IPA results indicate that community leadership
   and alternative material usage are categorized as successful programs
   exhibiting both high importance and high performance. Conversely,
   community self-reliance, product innovation, and packaging industry
   effectiveness emerged as priority gaps, revealing high public
   expectations coupled with weaker implementation performance.
   Additionally, workforce skills, feedback systems, and variety in policy
   instruments were classified as lower-priority areas, while investment in
   recycling infrastructure was identified as a potential area of resource
   over-allocation. Regression analysis reveals that factors such as
   environmental awareness, health concerns, higher educational attainment,
   female gender, consumer preference for low-plastic products, and
   perceived policy importance significantly enhance willingness to
   participate in plastic segregation programs. In contrast, perceived
   economic benefits and existing sustainable consumption habits exhibit
   negative correlations, indicating that awareness alone does not
   necessarily lead to active segregation behavior. The findings suggest
   that policymakers should prioritize the development of community-based
   leadership programs, enhance incentives for product innovation, improve
   household-level segregation systems, and expand affordable alternatives
   to single-use plastics.},
